1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
 In this lecture, I'm going to look at the 10th architecture

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,360
 consideration that we should think about while building

3
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:08,760
 or generative application.

4
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:12,920
 And that is model evaluation.

5
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:15,360
 So Amazon bedrock provides evaluation tools

6
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:19,720
 to evaluate, compare, and select the foundation model

7
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:24,440
 for your use case through Amazon bedrock model evaluation

8
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:26,120
 capability.

9
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:29,120
 Now, let's go ahead and understand how Amazon bedrock model

10
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:31,840
 evaluation capability works.

11
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:33,560
 So the first thing you need to do is you

12
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:37,440
 need to define your prompt data set.

13
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:38,440
 Now, let's take a scenario.

14
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:42,200
 Let's say you want to evaluate your foundation model.

15
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:45,720
 Maybe before you start building your generative application

16
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:48,840
 or once you've already built the generative application

17
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:53,840
 and you want to continuously evaluate its performance.

18
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:57,120
 So the first thing you will have to do is create a prompt data

19
00:00:57,120 --> 00:00:57,880
 set.

20
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:00,680
 So this is what your prompt data set could look like.

21
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:02,640
 So I only have three records, but you

22
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:04,920
 could have many more records.

23
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:07,320
 So the first thing we have to provide is the prompt.

24
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:11,720
 And here we are providing a prompt of the manufacturing

25
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:13,480
 wind turbine log.

26
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:17,360
 And then what we are saying is summarize this in one line.

27
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:19,720
 So you can see summarize this in one line.

28
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:22,760
 And then we are providing a reference response.

29
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:25,720
 So which means if the model is going to summarize this,

30
00:01:25,720 --> 00:01:29,920
 what should our reference response look like?

31
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:31,960
 OK, so you have the data set.

32
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:33,760
 It's in JSON l format.

33
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:37,240
 Then you need to upload it in the S3 bucket.

34
00:01:37,240 --> 00:01:40,880
 And you need to provide the bedrock model evaluation

35
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:44,640
 capability, the link to that prompt data set.

36
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:49,280
 So once you have uploaded this JSON l file in this S3 bucket,

37
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:52,480
 then you can select the model evaluation method.

38
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:55,280
 So it can be either programmatic, large language model

39
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:58,720
 as a judge or it can be human evaluation.

40
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:00,520
 And we're going to deep dive into each of them

41
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:01,640
 in a little bit.

42
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:05,000
 But once you have selected, let's say LLM as a judge,

43
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,240
 it's going to evaluate the response generated by the model

44
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:11,960
 and then generate a report.

45
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:13,880
 You can have a data scientist or an SME

46
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:16,880
 who can review the report.

47
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:19,640
 Now as we discussed, there are three evaluation approaches

48
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:20,400
 available.

49
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:24,360
 One is programmatic LLM as a judge and human evaluation.

50
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:26,400
 Now let's quickly go through each of them.

51
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:28,560
 So the first one is programmatic.

52
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:32,600
 It evaluates performance using just the model and metrics

53
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:34,360
 you select.

54
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:38,080
 So this is an inbuilt capability within Amazon bedrock,

55
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:41,160
 which is going to evaluate the model.

56
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:43,600
 So what do you need to do from your side?

57
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:45,720
 So you select the task type.

58
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:47,840
 So whether it's a use case for text generation,

59
00:02:47,840 --> 00:02:52,400
 text summarization, Q and A, or text classification.

60
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:55,360
 Then you select the metrics that you want to evaluate,

61
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:57,800
 accuracy, toxicity, and robustness.

62
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:00,200
 So those are our three metrics that are supported.

63
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:03,240
 Then either you can bring your own data set

64
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:06,640
 or you can use the built in data set.

65
00:03:06,640 --> 00:03:09,600
 So I already showed you what the data set is going to look like

66
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:11,520
 if you bring your own data set.

67
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:13,640
 Now this is what it's going to look like.

68
00:03:13,640 --> 00:03:16,800
 So here you define the evaluation name first.

69
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:19,760
 Then you select the model you want to evaluate.

70
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:21,520
 And here I have no pro.

71
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:24,520
 Then you define the task type, whether it's text generation,

72
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:26,240
 text summarization, and so on.

73
00:03:26,240 --> 00:03:27,960
 And then you define the still location

74
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:30,560
 where you're going to have your input data set

75
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:35,200
 and the output that would be generated by the service.

76
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:38,080
 Okay, now let's take a look at the LLM as a judge.

77
00:03:38,080 --> 00:03:42,680
 So a pre-trained model evaluates your model response using

78
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:44,160
 metrics you have selected.

79
00:03:44,160 --> 00:03:46,920
 So the key difference between automatic and LLM as a judge

80
00:03:46,920 --> 00:03:50,920
 is in the automatic AWS will have its own mechanism

81
00:03:50,920 --> 00:03:53,960
 to evaluate the performance of the foundation model,

82
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:56,280
 but in case of LLM as a judge,

83
00:03:56,280 --> 00:03:58,760
 you can select another foundation model.

84
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:01,720
 So let's say you're trying to evaluate Amazon Nova Pro

85
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:03,280
 for your use case.

86
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:07,840
 Then you can select any model from Anthropic or LAMA,

87
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:11,120
 evaluate the performance of the Amazon Nova Pro.

88
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:14,400
 So LLM as a judge, a pre-trained model evaluates your model

89
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:17,440
 performance using metrics you have selected.

90
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:19,400
 So what do you need to do from your side?

91
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:23,040
 You need to select the evaluator model and as we discussed,

92
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:26,200
 we can select Anthropic, LAMA and so on.

93
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:28,200
 Then inference source.

94
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:30,640
 So bedrock model that you want to evaluate.

95
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:34,360
 So you have to select the model that you want to evaluate

96
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:35,600
 for your use case.

97
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:37,080
 Then you define metrics.

98
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:39,440
 So you can define multiple metrics around quality,

99
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:42,000
 responsible AI, and then you can also create

100
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,240
 your own custom metrics.

101
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:46,880
 And then finally on the data set,

102
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:51,360
 either you can use the built-in or you can bring your own data

103
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:51,920
 set.

104
00:04:51,920 --> 00:04:55,120
 And we're going to see this when we do the hands-on.

105
00:04:55,120 --> 00:04:57,640
 OK, then finally we have the human evaluation.

106
00:04:57,640 --> 00:05:01,000
 So there are two ways in which you can do the human evaluation.

107
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,080
 First is AWS managed work team.

108
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:05,920
 So you can send an email to AWS and AWS

109
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:09,840
 will arrange a work team which can evaluate up to two models

110
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:13,760
 using a work team of your choice to provide feedback.

111
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:17,400
 Projects result based on the parameters that are specified

112
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:19,160
 while creating the evaluation.

113
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:21,160
 So basically just reach out to AWS.

114
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:23,360
 AWS will create a team.

115
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:25,080
 You give them the prompt data set.

116
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:26,400
 You give them the metrics.

117
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:28,720
 And then they will evaluate the model.

118
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:32,320
 The second one is human bring your own team.

119
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:35,160
 Evaluate response from up to two models

120
00:05:35,160 --> 00:05:36,680
 using your own work team.

121
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:38,400
 You can define evaluation metrics,

122
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:40,640
 specific to your job.

123
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:43,200
 Again, in this case, you have your own team.

124
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:45,480
 You provide them the prompt data set.

125
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:47,320
 You provide them the input data.

126
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:49,320
 You provide them the metrics.

127
00:05:49,320 --> 00:05:52,520
 And they will do the human evaluation.

128
00:05:52,520 --> 00:05:54,640
 OK, so that's pretty much it from this lecture.

129
00:05:54,640 --> 00:05:56,920
 As in the next lecture, thank you.

